h `--follow`** — no revision of any of the three ever held the pinned content. **So the attested code cannot be produced from git at all**, which is categorically worse than the stale case where the value still identifies real code. This is the fourth seeded shape exactly: *an artifact asserting a state it cannot have.* **The likely mechanism is not carelessness and that is the transferable part: the pin was computed against a tree the commit never held** — an uncommitted working-tree edit hashed before the commit that followed, or, more probably, a hash taken on the **cluster checkout**, which is forked from local by construction. **A content pin taken on one tree is meaningless in another**, and this campaign's own cluster/local fork rule already says the two are not comparable; nothing in the receipt format records which tree it was taken on. That makes it a process fix rather than a correction. **Rules.** (i) A content pin must record the tree and commit it was taken against, or it cannot be distinguished later from one taken against nothing. (ii) **Distinguish STALE from DANGLING before assigning a disposition** — stale implies no re-run (the code is at a named commit), dangling implies the attestation is unrecoverable; the two look identical to a hash comparison and differ only under a history walk. (iii) **Never hand-edit either kind**: for a stale pin the value identifies the code actually run, for a dangling one it is the only surviving evidence the content ever differed. (iv) A pin nothing reads cannot report its own break — 71 of these had been false for weeks with no mechanism capable of noticing. **LIMITS, stated because the number invites over-reading:** 29 pinned paths are untracked (scratch/cluster) and were **not checked**, and the pairing heuristic needs a path key beside the sha key, so this covers **338 of the 884** hash-valued fields — **38%; the other 62% are unswept.** Disposition NOT taken and not mine: the two PET receipts are Session C's by